speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, I
entirely agree with what you have said. I am not discussing the facts of the case;
I particularly said so. I am not discussing whether or not there is justification for the prosecution, or what the courts will do. I am discussing the question of freedom of the press in this country, pointing to the use of the machinery of the law in a way contrary to that which the experience of democratic peoples has shown is necessary in order to preserve freedom, and I am suggesting that this is something that deserves the attention of the House of Commons, which is the custodian of the freedoms and liberties of the people. Where wrong has been done, let it be punished; but the law says, and those who sat in parliament in 1888, decided, that prosecution for defamatory libel should take place where the libel was committed or where the editor or publisher lives. That, sir, is in this province of Ontario, and nowhere else. For that reason I submit that I am within the rules; and I await your ruling.
